# Project: PDF Viewer SDK

## What we are building

A client-side-only PDF viewer SDK web app, built as a Vite + React + TypeScript
single-page application. The deliverable is the viewer SDK itself — not the
host application that embeds it. Users land on a file-picker entry screen,
choose a PDF (local file or bundled sample), and the viewer mounts inline.
The viewer supports rendering, navigation, zoom, three view modes, a Document
Editor mode with page operations, printing, and local save.

This is a take-home assignment. The repo has three top-level folders required
by the spec:
  A/  — the working MVP source code
  B/  — architecture and design document
  C/  — Cursor AI usage log

All application source lives inside A/. Inside A/ the layout is:
  A/src/engine/      — PdfEngine interface + adapters
  A/src/model/       — DocumentModel (pdf-lib wrapper)
  A/src/components/  — React components
  A/src/context/     — React Context providers + reducers
  A/src/hooks/       — custom hooks
  A/src/types/       — shared TypeScript types
  A/public/samples/  — bundled sample PDFs

The bonus WebAssembly tier is enabled. The MuPdfEngine adapter is implemented
alongside PdfJsEngine. The bonus tier adds three features gated on the MuPDF
engine being the active engine: rectangle redaction with true byte removal
(not just a visual overlay), text annotations placed by clicking on a page,
and bookmark read/write. Text-highlighter redaction, signature drawing, and
full PDF form widget interaction are deliberately out of scope even within
the bonus tier — they are too deep for the time budget.

## Stack

- TypeScript strict mode. No `any` types unless wrapping an untyped library.
- React 18 with functional components and hooks. No class components.
- Vite for dev server and bundling. Dev server must set COOP/COEP headers.
- pdfjs-dist for default PDF rendering.
- pdf-lib for all editing and export.
- mupdf (WASM) for the bonus tier. Lazy-imported via dynamic import() so it
  does not bloat the initial bundle when PDF.js is selected. Loaded only when
  the user explicitly picks the MuPDF engine on the file picker screen.
- CSS Modules for styling. No Tailwind, styled-components, or CSS-in-JS runtime.
- Vitest for unit tests. Playwright if E2E is added later.

The approved dependency list is the eight items above plus their type packages.
Do not introduce any other runtime dependency without being asked. No date
libraries, no utility libraries, no UI component libraries, no icon libraries,
no drag-and-drop libraries — write what's needed inline.

## Architecture rules

- All PDF rendering goes through the PdfEngine interface in src/engine/types.ts.
  Two adapters exist: PdfJsEngine (default) and MuPdfEngine (bonus tier).
  No component imports pdfjs-dist or mupdf directly — only via the engine.
- All PDF editing (rotate, reorder, merge, extract, save) goes through the
  DocumentModel class in src/model/. Rendering and editing are separate concerns
  with separate lifecycles. Never merge them.
- State is managed by React Context + useReducer. There are TWO contexts:
    ViewerContext — current page, zoom, fit mode, view mode, loading progress
    EditorContext — page list, selection, undo/redo stack, dirty flag
  This split prevents the editor from re-rendering on scroll-driven page changes.
  Do not collapse them into a single context.
- Sets and Maps in state must be replaced (not mutated) on every reducer update.
  React's useReducer compares with Object.is — mutating in place skips renders.
- Edit operations happen via a useDocumentEditor hook that wraps DocumentModel,
  saves bytes after each mutation, reloads the engine from the new bytes, and
  pushes a snapshot to the undo stack. Never mutate the engine in place.
- Page numbers in public APIs are 1-indexed. pdf-lib uses 0-indexed internally.
  DocumentModel handles the conversion. Never expose 0-indexed numbers to UI.
- Render with devicePixelRatio so canvases are crisp on retina displays.
- Continuous scroll mode is virtualized: only pages in the viewport ±1 page
  are rendered to canvas. Off-screen pages are empty divs of correct height.
- All async render calls accept an AbortSignal and cancel cleanly when
  superseded by a newer render on the same canvas.
- The active engine is chosen at file-load time, controlled by an
  `enginePreference: 'pdfjs' | 'mupdf'` field on AppState. The user picks
  via a toggle on the file picker entry screen, and the choice persists
  in localStorage so it survives reloads.
- The Vite dev server MUST set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp headers. MuPDF requires
  SharedArrayBuffer, which requires these headers. This is non-optional
  now that the bonus tier is in scope. Configure these in vite.config.ts
  via the server.headers option.
- Bonus features (annotations, redaction, bookmarks write) MUST be disabled
  in the UI when the active engine is PdfJsEngine. Show disabled buttons
  with a tooltip explaining "Switch to MuPDF engine to enable this feature."
  Never silently no-op a click.

## Scope guardrails — DO NOT add these

The following were considered and explicitly excluded from scope. If you find
yourself proposing them, stop and ask first.

- Do NOT build a records management UI. No RecordListView, RecordCard,
  RecordDetailView, FileCard, or static records data file. The user lands
  directly on a FilePicker entry screen.
- Do NOT add a left thumbnail sidebar to the viewer. It is not in the demo
  video and not required. The viewer has a single-row top toolbar and a
  centered canvas, nothing else.
- Do NOT build an ImportDialog modal with page selection. Import opens a
  native file picker and merges all pages of the chosen file at the position
  after the currently selected page.
- Do NOT add a backend, API server, user accounts, authentication, or any
  server-side persistence. Everything runs client-side.
- Do NOT add features beyond what the current build phase asks for. If the
  phase says "build the toolbar," do not also add keyboard shortcuts in the
  same turn — that's a later phase.
- Do NOT implement text-highlighter redaction. Only rectangle redaction.
  Text-highlighter requires text-layer hit testing and selection state
  management — too deep for the time budget.
- Do NOT implement signature drawing or signature creation flows. Detecting
  and rendering existing signature widget annotations as read-only is fine;
  letting users draw or place signatures is out of scope.
- Do NOT implement interactive PDF form widgets (checkboxes, text inputs,
  radio buttons). If the engine surfaces them, render them as static visuals.
  Do not wire them to user input.
- Do NOT eagerly load mupdf on app start. It's a ~10 MB WASM binary. Use
  dynamic import() inside MuPdfEngine methods so the WASM is only fetched
  when the user actually selects MuPDF as their engine.

## Viewer toolbar — exact button order

The viewer toolbar is a single horizontal row. Buttons in this order, from
left to right:

  1. View-mode dropdown (grid icon + chevron) — Continuous / Single / Spread
  2. Print
  3. Download
  4. Edit Pages (pen icon) — toggles editor mode, shows active state when on
  5. Refresh
  6. Upload
  7. Vertical divider
  8. "Page" label, prev arrow, page input, next arrow, "X / N" total
  9. Vertical divider
  10. Document icon (fit-to-page)
  11. Zoom out
  12. Zoom level readout (e.g. "100%")
  13. Zoom in
  14. Vertical divider
  15. Rotate left
  16. Rotate right

## Editor toolbar — exact button order

When editor mode is active, a second toolbar appears below the viewer toolbar
with these buttons in order:

  Scan | Import | Delete Pages | Rotate Pages Left | Rotate Pages Right |
  Extract Pages | Undo | Redo | Zoom Out | Zoom In | Select None |
  Copy Pages | Paste Pages

Scan is a stub that logs to console — note this as a known limitation in
the architecture doc. Editor Zoom In/Out controls thumbnail size in the page
grid, separate from the viewer's document zoom.

## Visual style

Match the demo video's flat teal/blue-grey palette:

  Primary color:           #2d6a7a   (active buttons, progress bars, links)
  Primary hover:           #245864
  Page canvas background:  #e8eef2   (pale blue-grey behind the page)
  Toolbar background:      #ffffff
  Border color:            #d4dce2
  Active button background: #d8e8ec  with #2d6a7a border

Single-row toolbars, 32px icon buttons, 8px gaps. No gradients, no shadows
beyond focus rings, no rounded corners larger than 6px. Filename appears
top-left next to a "Go Back" link when a document is open.

## Code style

- TypeScript strict: noImplicitAny, strictNullChecks, noUncheckedIndexedAccess.
- Files capped at 400 lines. Split aggressively when approaching the limit.
- One component per file. Co-locate the .module.css file next to the .tsx.
- Imports in this order: external packages, internal absolute imports
  (@/types, @/engine, etc.), relative imports, CSS modules.
- Every interactive element has an aria-label. Buttons get :focus-visible
  outline rings using the primary color.
- Custom hooks named useX, dispatched actions named in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE,
  components in PascalCase, files match the export name.
- No console.log in committed code except where explicitly noted as a stub
  (e.g. the Scan button).

## What NOT to do

- No `position: fixed` for toolbars or panels — use sticky or normal flow.
- No eager rendering of all pages in continuous mode — always virtualize.
- No mutation of state Sets/Maps — always create new instances.
- No direct pdfjs-dist or mupdf imports outside src/engine/.
- No direct pdf-lib imports outside src/model/.
- No new dependencies without being asked.
- No files larger than 400 lines.
- No re-introduction of records UI, thumbnail sidebar, or import dialog.